
#  What is N+1 problem in hibernate and how to solve it?

   The N+1 problem refers to a performance issue that arises when using an ORM framework like Hibernate.

   For each entity fetched, an additional query is executed to fetch its associated entities,
   Hibernate executes 1 query to retrieve the parent entity and N queries to retrieve the child entities.

   Example:
   - Two entities are there, Author and Book which have a one-to-many relationship
   - One author can have multiple boos.
   - Retrieving through a list of authors and then iterating through each author to access their books results
     in N+1 queries
   - N represents number of authors
   - Hibernate generates a special SQL query for each author's books in this scenario


   Ways to solve N+1 problem
   - Eager Fetching
   - Fetch Joins
   - Subselect Fetching
   - Second level cache
   - Entity Graphs
   - Batch Fetching

##   Eager Fetching
     Can be used to load associated entities in a single query, reducing the number of DB hits

     @OneToMany(fetch = FetchType.EAGER)
     @JoinColumn(name = "author_id")
     private List <Book> books;

###  Fetch Joins

     Explicitly tells Hibernate to fetch associated entities in the same query using a JOIN,
     avoiding the N+1 problem entirely.

     Works well when you know upfront that you'll need the associated data.
     Downside: can cause duplicate rows (cartesian product) when fetching multiple collections at once —
     use DISTINCT or a Set instead of List to avoid duplicates.

     @Query("SELECT a FROM Author a JOIN FETCH a.books")
     List<Author> findAllWithBooks();
####
    Subselect Fetching

    Instead of firing one query per parent, Hibernate fires a second query that fetches
    all the associated children for all the parents already loaded, using a subselect.
    Converts N+1 queries into 2 queries total: one for authors, one subselect for all books belonging to those authors.
    Good when you're loading a list of parents and know you'll touch their children.

    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "author", fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
    @Fetch(FetchMode.SUBSELECT)
    private List<Book> books;
#####
    Second Level Cache

    Caches entities across sessions (not just within one Hibernate session like the first-level cache),
    so repeated access to the same associations doesn't hit the DB again.
    Requires a cache provider (Ehcache, Infinispan, etc.).
    Doesn't eliminate N+1 on first load, but avoids repeated N+1 hits for data that doesn't change often.

    @Cacheable
    @Cache(usage = CacheConcurrencyStrategy.READ_WRITE)
    public class Author { ... }
######
    Entity Graphs

    Lets you define, at the query level, which associations should be eagerly fetched —
    without changing the entity's default fetch type globally.
    More flexible than hardcoding FetchType.EAGER on the entity, since you can choose per-query whether to
    fetch the association.

    @EntityGraph(attributePaths = {"books"})
    @Query("SELECT a FROM Author a")
    List<Author> findAllWithBooksGraph();
######
    Batch Fetching

    Instead of firing one query per parent for its children, Hibernate batches multiple parents into a single IN query.
    Converts N queries into N/batchSize queries (e.g., 100 authors with batch size 10 → 10 queries instead of 100).
    Good middle ground when eager fetching everything isn't desirable but N+1 is still a problem.

    hibernate.default_batch_fetch_size=10